<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazanlak</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kazanlak is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, in the Valley of Roses south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a>. Nearby lie the remains of the Odrysian royal city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazanlak-tomb">Kazanlak Tomb</a>, a Thracian tomb with wall paintings of about 300 BCE that is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The surrounding valley, often called the Valley of the Thracian Kings, holds dozens of rich burial mounds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kazanlak.jpg" alt="Kazanlak" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: stealman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kazanlak_Center.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kazanlak lies in Stara Zagora Province, in the middle of the Kazanlak Valley at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a>. The valley formed in the Quaternary Period, when the Balkan and Sredna Gora ranges rose, and hot mineral springs near Ovoshtnik, Yagoda and Pavel Banya mark its fault line. It is drained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tundzha">Tundzha</a> river, which rises in the highest part of the Balkan east of Mount Botev and flows on to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a>. The average altitude is 350 m, and the soils suit oil-producing crops and herbs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest settlement in the area of the modern town dates from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, in the sixth to fifth millennium BCE. In the fourth and third centuries BCE the lands on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tundzha">Tundzha</a> lay within the dominion of the Thracian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-iii">Seuthes III</a> and held an important place in Hellenistic Thrace. The Thracian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthopolis">Seuthopolis</a> was uncovered near Kazanlak when the Koprinka Reservoir was built. Close to it stands a Thracian tomb with a vaulted brick tholos, a narrow corridor and a round burial chamber, painted with murals of a Thracian couple at a ritual funeral feast. It belongs to a large necropolis, in a region where more than a thousand tombs of kings and aristocrats are found.</p><p>After the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> the town numbered about 9,000 people in the 1880s, and it grew steadily by migration from the countryside, passing 60,000 at its peak in 1985. The Iskra chitalishte, a reading centre founded in 1860, holds a library, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, cinema and museum, and it hosted the first Bulgarian opera, Siromahkinya. The Iskra Town History Museum was founded on 29 June 1901 by Peter Topuzov.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kazanlak is the centre of rose oil extraction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and the oil-producing rose of Kazanlak is one of the country's widely recognised national symbols. The town's Rose Museum began as a small exhibition in 1967 and became an independent museum in 1969. The Thracian tomb has been on the UNESCO World Heritage list since 1979. Nearby, the Shipka National Park preserves the battlefield of the Russian-Turkish Liberation War of the 1870s, with the Freedom Monument opened in 1934.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kazanlak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kazanlak Tomb, with wall paintings of a funeral feast and a chariot race from around 300 BCE, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and the area around <b>Kazanlak</b>, often called the Valley of the Thracian Kings, contains dozens of rich mound tombs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Excavations in Bulgaria have uncovered tombs and sanctuaries such as the Tomb of <b>Kazanlak</b> in the Valley of the Thracian Rulers, the Getic burial complex and the Tomb of Sveshtari, and the sites of Tatul and Perperikon.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
